1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,554
Somewhere in your house,

2
00:00:01,554 --> 00:00:02,914
there's a photograph.

3
00:00:03,094 --> 00:00:04,960
A young couple on the steps of

4
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,520
a church.

5
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:06,710
She's in white,

6
00:00:06,710 --> 00:00:09,334
holding a bouquet a little too tightly.

7
00:00:09,514 --> 00:00:11,203
He's in a dark suit with a

8
00:00:11,203 --> 00:00:12,697
carnation on the lapel,

9
00:00:12,697 --> 00:00:15,296
grinning like he can't believe his luck.

10
00:00:15,476 --> 00:00:17,902
The year is nineteen sixty-five.

11
00:00:18,082 --> 00:00:20,356
And they have absolutely no idea what's

12
00:00:20,356 --> 00:00:20,706
ahead.

13
00:00:21,057 --> 00:00:22,820
In nineteen sixty-five,

14
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:25,963
the typical bride was around twenty years

15
00:00:25,963 --> 00:00:26,269
old.

16
00:00:26,449 --> 00:00:29,305
The typical groom was around twenty-three.

17
00:00:29,485 --> 00:00:31,476
Many of them had been dating since

18
00:00:31,476 --> 00:00:32,179
high school.

19
00:00:32,359 --> 00:00:34,159
And a wedding wasn't just a party.

20
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:36,393
It was the single biggest step in

21
00:00:36,393 --> 00:00:37,763
a young person's life,

22
00:00:37,763 --> 00:00:40,129
the day they left their parents' house

23
00:00:40,129 --> 00:00:40,689
for good.

24
00:00:41,039 --> 00:00:43,160
So let's go back and walk through

25
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:45,602
it, from the proposal to the honeymoon

26
00:00:45,602 --> 00:00:47,529
to the first little apartment.

27
00:00:47,709 --> 00:00:49,488
The shower, the church,

28
00:00:49,488 --> 00:00:51,498
the reception in the hall,

29
00:00:51,498 --> 00:00:54,513
and all the things nobody does anymore.

30
00:00:54,693 --> 00:00:56,870
This is what made your wedding day

31
00:00:56,870 --> 00:00:59,047
in nineteen sixty-five so special.

32
00:00:59,397 --> 00:01:01,759
It usually started with a question to

33
00:01:01,759 --> 00:01:02,462
her father.

34
00:01:02,642 --> 00:01:04,690
Before a young man proposed,

35
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:07,105
he was expected to ask her father

36
00:01:07,105 --> 00:01:08,203
for permission.

37
00:01:08,383 --> 00:01:09,530
He'd put on a tie,

38
00:01:09,530 --> 00:01:10,996
sit in the living room,

39
00:01:10,996 --> 00:01:13,099
and try to explain how he planned

40
00:01:13,099 --> 00:01:14,629
to support his daughter.

41
00:01:14,809 --> 00:01:17,288
More than one young man remembered that

42
00:01:17,288 --> 00:01:20,211
conversation as the most frightening half hour

43
00:01:20,211 --> 00:01:20,974
of his life.

44
00:01:21,323 --> 00:01:23,576
Then came the proposal itself.

45
00:01:23,756 --> 00:01:25,172
Often on bended knee,

46
00:01:25,172 --> 00:01:27,329
sometimes in a parked car at the

47
00:01:27,329 --> 00:01:29,824
lake, sometimes on the porch swing at

48
00:01:29,824 --> 00:01:31,105
her parents' house.

49
00:01:31,285 --> 00:01:33,154
The ring was usually modest.

50
00:01:33,334 --> 00:01:35,603
A small diamond on a thin band,

51
00:01:35,603 --> 00:01:38,384
bought on an installment plan from the

52
00:01:38,384 --> 00:01:40,068
jewelry store downtown.

53
00:01:40,248 --> 00:01:42,518
She showed it to everybody she knew

54
00:01:42,518 --> 00:01:43,232
for months.

55
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:46,395
The engagement was announced in the local

56
00:01:46,395 --> 00:01:49,621
newspaper, with a photograph of the bride-to-be

57
00:01:49,621 --> 00:01:52,229
and a few sentences about her parents,

58
00:01:52,229 --> 00:01:54,425
her school and her fiancé's job.

59
00:01:54,605 --> 00:01:57,694
Mothers cut out those announcements and kept

60
00:01:57,694 --> 00:01:59,098
them in a scrapbook.

61
00:01:59,278 --> 00:02:01,177
Many families still have them,

62
00:02:01,177 --> 00:02:02,506
yellowed and brittle,

63
00:02:02,506 --> 00:02:03,899
in a drawer somewhere.

64
00:02:04,248 --> 00:02:06,471
And many brides had been preparing for

65
00:02:06,471 --> 00:02:07,348
this for years.

66
00:02:07,528 --> 00:02:08,502
A hope chest,

67
00:02:08,502 --> 00:02:10,899
often a cedar chest given to her

68
00:02:10,899 --> 00:02:13,671
at sixteen, held the things she would

69
00:02:13,671 --> 00:02:15,319
need for her new home.

70
00:02:15,499 --> 00:02:18,125
Embroidered pillowcases, tablecloths,

71
00:02:18,125 --> 00:02:21,106
dish towels, a quilt from her grandmother.

72
00:02:21,286 --> 00:02:23,572
Some of those chests still sit at

73
00:02:23,572 --> 00:02:25,373
the foot of the bed today.

74
00:02:25,722 --> 00:02:27,789
Engagements were often short.

75
00:02:27,969 --> 00:02:30,152
Six months, maybe a year.

76
00:02:30,332 --> 00:02:32,236
There wasn't much reason to wait.

77
00:02:32,416 --> 00:02:34,555
Many young men had a steady job,

78
00:02:34,555 --> 00:02:37,496
the girls had been planning since childhood,

79
00:02:37,496 --> 00:02:39,167
and in a lot of families,

80
00:02:39,167 --> 00:02:41,975
a couple simply didn't live together until

81
00:02:41,975 --> 00:02:43,178
they were married.

82
00:02:43,358 --> 00:02:44,600
So the date was set,

83
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,214
and the church was booked.

84
00:02:46,564 --> 00:02:49,336
Before the wedding came the bridal shower,

85
00:02:49,336 --> 00:02:51,777
given by the bride's friends or aunts

86
00:02:51,777 --> 00:02:54,350
in somebody's living room or the church

87
00:02:54,350 --> 00:02:54,680
hall.

88
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:57,484
The ladies wore hats and gloves.

89
00:02:57,664 --> 00:02:59,487
There were finger sandwiches,

90
00:02:59,487 --> 00:03:00,871
punch in a glass bowl,

91
00:03:00,871 --> 00:03:03,260
and games where the guests guessed how

92
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:04,769
many pins were in a jar.

93
00:03:04,949 --> 00:03:07,509
And the bride opened every present while

94
00:03:07,509 --> 00:03:09,686
somebody wrote down who gave what.

95
00:03:10,036 --> 00:03:11,394
The gifts were practical.

96
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:14,428
Toasters, electric percolators,

97
00:03:14,428 --> 00:03:16,822
mixing bowls, bath towels,

98
00:03:16,822 --> 00:03:19,584
sheets and a set of good china

99
00:03:19,584 --> 00:03:20,781
for holidays.

100
00:03:20,961 --> 00:03:24,150
Department stores had started bridal registries,

101
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:26,475
where the bride chose her china and

102
00:03:26,475 --> 00:03:29,598
silver patterns, and relatives bought one place

103
00:03:29,598 --> 00:03:30,794
setting at a time.

104
00:03:30,974 --> 00:03:33,518
Some couples didn't complete the set for

105
00:03:33,518 --> 00:03:33,900
years.

106
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:36,200
The dress was a big decision.

107
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:38,551
Some brides bought a gown at a

108
00:03:38,551 --> 00:03:40,722
department store bridal salon.

109
00:03:40,902 --> 00:03:43,030
Many wore their mother's dress,

110
00:03:43,030 --> 00:03:44,060
altered to fit.

111
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:46,170
And a great many had their dress

112
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:47,196
sewn by a mother,

113
00:03:47,196 --> 00:03:48,945
an aunt or a neighbor who was

114
00:03:48,945 --> 00:03:50,091
good with a needle.

115
00:03:50,271 --> 00:03:51,891
Long sleeves, a high neck,

116
00:03:51,891 --> 00:03:54,010
and a veil that reached the floor.

117
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:55,211
For the men,

118
00:03:55,211 --> 00:03:57,268
there was the bachelor party,

119
00:03:57,268 --> 00:04:00,247
which in nineteen sixty-five was usually a

120
00:04:00,247 --> 00:04:02,801
few beers with friends at the corner

121
00:04:02,801 --> 00:04:05,354
tavern, or a card game in somebody's

122
00:04:05,354 --> 00:04:05,993
basement.

123
00:04:06,173 --> 00:04:07,496
Nothing too wild.

124
00:04:07,676 --> 00:04:09,519
Everybody had to be in church on

125
00:04:09,519 --> 00:04:11,996
Saturday morning, and his mother would know

126
00:04:11,996 --> 00:04:13,493
if he wasn't feeling well.

127
00:04:13,843 --> 00:04:16,298
The bride and her mother addressed the

128
00:04:16,298 --> 00:04:18,753
invitations by hand at the dining room

129
00:04:18,753 --> 00:04:20,820
table, in their best penmanship.

130
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,995
Engraved cards, with a tiny sheet of

131
00:04:23,995 --> 00:04:26,906
tissue paper inside to keep the ink

132
00:04:26,906 --> 00:04:28,071
from smudging.

133
00:04:28,251 --> 00:04:30,794
Every relative had to be invited,

134
00:04:30,794 --> 00:04:33,877
including the cousins nobody had seen in

135
00:04:33,877 --> 00:04:34,648
ten years.

136
00:04:34,828 --> 00:04:37,282
Leaving someone out could start a family

137
00:04:37,282 --> 00:04:39,123
feud that lasted a generation.

138
00:04:39,474 --> 00:04:40,513
The night before,

139
00:04:40,513 --> 00:04:42,776
there was the rehearsal at the church

140
00:04:42,776 --> 00:04:43,877
and then a dinner,

141
00:04:43,877 --> 00:04:46,323
usually paid for by the groom's parents.

142
00:04:46,503 --> 00:04:48,483
The two families sat together,

143
00:04:48,483 --> 00:04:50,396
sometimes for the first time,

144
00:04:50,396 --> 00:04:51,914
and tried to get along.

145
00:04:52,094 --> 00:04:54,903
The groom's grandmother looked over the bride.

146
00:04:55,083 --> 00:04:57,521
The bride's father looked over the groom.

147
00:04:57,701 --> 00:05:00,140
Everybody smiled a little too much.

148
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:01,774
On the wedding day,

149
00:05:01,774 --> 00:05:04,274
almost everybody married in a church.

150
00:05:04,454 --> 00:05:06,776
Saturday morning or early afternoon,

151
00:05:06,776 --> 00:05:08,646
in June if they could get it.

152
00:05:08,826 --> 00:05:11,530
The church was decorated with white ribbons

153
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:13,040
on the ends of the pews.

154
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:15,724
The organist played softly as the guests

155
00:05:15,724 --> 00:05:18,165
arrived, the bride's family on the left

156
00:05:18,165 --> 00:05:20,418
and the groom's family on the right.

157
00:05:20,769 --> 00:05:22,899
The bride came down the aisle on

158
00:05:22,899 --> 00:05:24,031
her father's arm.

159
00:05:24,211 --> 00:05:25,825
Some fathers cried.

160
00:05:26,005 --> 00:05:27,235
Most tried not to.

161
00:05:27,415 --> 00:05:28,340
At the altar,

162
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:29,691
he lifted her veil,

163
00:05:29,691 --> 00:05:30,900
kissed her cheek,

164
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:33,318
and placed her hand in the groom's

165
00:05:33,318 --> 00:05:33,673
hand.

166
00:05:33,853 --> 00:05:36,115
Then he sat down beside his wife,

167
00:05:36,115 --> 00:05:37,417
and in that moment,

168
00:05:37,417 --> 00:05:40,501
everybody understood that the family had just

169
00:05:40,501 --> 00:05:41,597
changed forever.

170
00:05:41,947 --> 00:05:43,363
The vows were traditional.

171
00:05:43,543 --> 00:05:45,124
To have and to hold,

172
00:05:45,124 --> 00:05:47,022
for better or for worse,

173
00:05:47,022 --> 00:05:48,999
for richer or for poorer,

174
00:05:48,999 --> 00:05:51,055
in sickness and in health.

175
00:05:51,235 --> 00:05:53,986
Many brides still promised to obey.

176
00:05:54,166 --> 00:05:55,793
The ceremony was short,

177
00:05:55,793 --> 00:05:57,277
the kiss was shorter,

178
00:05:57,277 --> 00:05:59,823
and then the couple walked back down

179
00:05:59,823 --> 00:06:01,944
the aisle as husband and wife,

180
00:06:01,944 --> 00:06:04,278
while the organ played as loud as

181
00:06:04,278 --> 00:06:05,126
it could go.

182
00:06:05,476 --> 00:06:08,170
Outside, the guests threw rice as the

183
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:10,572
couple ran down the church steps.

184
00:06:10,752 --> 00:06:13,031
Someone had tied tin cans and old

185
00:06:13,031 --> 00:06:15,171
shoes to the back bumper of the

186
00:06:15,171 --> 00:06:17,933
car, and written Just Married in shaving

187
00:06:17,933 --> 00:06:19,936
cream across the rear window.

188
00:06:20,116 --> 00:06:22,294
The couple drove off honking the horn,

189
00:06:22,294 --> 00:06:24,644
with the whole town knowing exactly where

190
00:06:24,644 --> 00:06:25,561
they were going.

191
00:06:25,911 --> 00:06:28,999
The wedding photographer was usually a man

192
00:06:28,999 --> 00:06:30,616
from the local studio,

193
00:06:30,616 --> 00:06:32,749
with a big flash camera and a

194
00:06:32,749 --> 00:06:33,631
bag of film.

195
00:06:33,811 --> 00:06:35,761
He took a few dozen pictures,

196
00:06:35,761 --> 00:06:37,039
not a few thousand.

197
00:06:37,219 --> 00:06:38,844
The couple on the steps.

198
00:06:39,024 --> 00:06:40,278
The cutting of the cake.

199
00:06:40,458 --> 00:06:42,954
The families lined up in rows.

200
00:06:43,134 --> 00:06:44,577
Every shot counted,

201
00:06:44,577 --> 00:06:47,006
and nobody saw any of them until

202
00:06:47,006 --> 00:06:47,918
weeks later.

203
00:06:48,268 --> 00:06:50,427
The reception was usually simple.

204
00:06:50,607 --> 00:06:51,919
The church hall,

205
00:06:51,919 --> 00:06:53,068
the fire hall,

206
00:06:53,068 --> 00:06:55,200
the VFW or the union hall,

207
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:58,973
decorated with paper streamers and crepe paper

208
00:06:58,973 --> 00:06:59,465
bells.

209
00:06:59,645 --> 00:07:02,338
Ladies from the church or the family

210
00:07:02,338 --> 00:07:03,535
cooked the food.

211
00:07:03,715 --> 00:07:06,737
A wedding in nineteen sixty-five didn't cost

212
00:07:06,737 --> 00:07:08,591
the fortune it costs today.

213
00:07:08,771 --> 00:07:11,027
It cost what the bride's parents could

214
00:07:11,027 --> 00:07:12,985
afford, and everybody chipped in.

215
00:07:13,336 --> 00:07:15,391
The food depended on where you lived.

216
00:07:15,571 --> 00:07:18,640
Ham sandwiches and potato salad in the

217
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:19,286
Midwest.

218
00:07:19,466 --> 00:07:21,196
Fried chicken in the South.

219
00:07:21,376 --> 00:07:24,502
Platters of homemade pierogi and kielbasa in

220
00:07:24,502 --> 00:07:27,913
Polish neighborhoods, and trays of cookies baked

221
00:07:27,913 --> 00:07:30,044
by every aunt in the family in

222
00:07:30,044 --> 00:07:30,826
Pittsburgh.

223
00:07:31,006 --> 00:07:33,891
In parts of New Jersey and Philadelphia,

224
00:07:33,891 --> 00:07:36,920
there were so many wrapped sandwiches that

225
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:39,516
people called it a football wedding.

226
00:07:39,867 --> 00:07:42,072
The wedding cake was the centerpiece.

227
00:07:42,252 --> 00:07:44,709
Three or four tiers of white cake,

228
00:07:44,709 --> 00:07:46,588
covered in white frosting,

229
00:07:46,588 --> 00:07:49,189
with a little bride and groom figure

230
00:07:49,189 --> 00:07:49,694
on top.

231
00:07:49,874 --> 00:07:52,246
The couple cut the first piece together,

232
00:07:52,246 --> 00:07:53,787
and fed each other a bite.

233
00:07:53,967 --> 00:07:56,041
And the top tier was wrapped up

234
00:07:56,041 --> 00:07:57,580
and put in the freezer,

235
00:07:57,580 --> 00:08:00,190
to be eaten on their first anniversary.

236
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:01,480
Then the dancing.

237
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:03,082
Sometimes a band,

238
00:08:03,082 --> 00:08:06,176
sometimes a record player and a stack

239
00:08:06,176 --> 00:08:07,012
of albums.

240
00:08:07,192 --> 00:08:09,338
The first dance was the bride and

241
00:08:09,338 --> 00:08:11,743
groom alone, while everybody watched.

242
00:08:11,923 --> 00:08:13,967
Then the bride danced with her father,

243
00:08:13,967 --> 00:08:15,580
and the groom with his mother.

244
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:17,882
And then the whole room joined in,

245
00:08:17,882 --> 00:08:20,439
from the grandparents to the flower girl.

246
00:08:20,790 --> 00:08:22,975
The bride tossed her bouquet over her

247
00:08:22,975 --> 00:08:24,688
shoulder to the single girls,

248
00:08:24,688 --> 00:08:26,696
who pretended not to care and then

249
00:08:26,696 --> 00:08:27,523
fought for it.

250
00:08:27,703 --> 00:08:29,625
The groom took off her garter and

251
00:08:29,625 --> 00:08:31,198
threw it to the single men.

252
00:08:31,378 --> 00:08:33,157
And by the end of the night,

253
00:08:33,157 --> 00:08:34,682
the uncles were singing,

254
00:08:34,682 --> 00:08:36,906
the children were asleep on rows of

255
00:08:36,906 --> 00:08:39,575
folding chairs, and the bride's mother was

256
00:08:39,575 --> 00:08:40,910
finally sitting down.

257
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:43,250
Guests rarely gave a gift at the

258
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:43,872
reception.

259
00:08:44,052 --> 00:08:46,334
They had already sent presents to the

260
00:08:46,334 --> 00:08:47,198
bride's house.

261
00:08:47,378 --> 00:08:48,946
But in many communities,

262
00:08:48,946 --> 00:08:51,493
especially Italian and Polish families,

263
00:08:51,493 --> 00:08:53,191
there was the money dance,

264
00:08:53,191 --> 00:08:55,738
where guests pinned dollar bills to the

265
00:08:55,738 --> 00:08:57,763
bride's dress for a turn on the

266
00:08:57,763 --> 00:08:58,547
dance floor.

267
00:08:58,727 --> 00:09:00,572
Or there was an envelope,

268
00:09:00,572 --> 00:09:03,671
slipped quietly into the groom's pocket by

269
00:09:03,671 --> 00:09:04,335
an uncle.

270
00:09:04,685 --> 00:09:05,940
After the reception,

271
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:09,015
the couple changed into their going-away clothes.

272
00:09:09,195 --> 00:09:11,047
She wore a nice suit and a

273
00:09:11,047 --> 00:09:11,331
hat.

274
00:09:11,511 --> 00:09:13,334
He wore a jacket and tie.

275
00:09:13,514 --> 00:09:15,864
They said goodbye to everyone at the

276
00:09:15,864 --> 00:09:18,344
door, and drove away into the evening.

277
00:09:18,524 --> 00:09:19,570
For many of them,

278
00:09:19,570 --> 00:09:21,477
it was the first night they had

279
00:09:21,477 --> 00:09:24,062
ever spent away from their parents' house.

280
00:09:24,412 --> 00:09:27,394
The honeymoon was usually short and close

281
00:09:27,394 --> 00:09:27,976
to home.

282
00:09:28,156 --> 00:09:31,690
Niagara Falls was the most famous destination

283
00:09:31,690 --> 00:09:33,967
of all, and the town was full

284
00:09:33,967 --> 00:09:36,480
of motels with honeymoon suites.

285
00:09:36,660 --> 00:09:40,439
The Pocono Mountains in Pennsylvania had resorts

286
00:09:40,439 --> 00:09:42,407
built just for newlyweds.

287
00:09:42,587 --> 00:09:44,893
Others went to a lake cabin,

288
00:09:44,893 --> 00:09:47,198
the beach, or a big hotel in

289
00:09:47,198 --> 00:09:47,939
the city.

290
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:50,687
Some couples flew for the first time

291
00:09:50,687 --> 00:09:51,953
on their honeymoon,

292
00:09:51,953 --> 00:09:53,817
maybe to Florida or Bermuda,

293
00:09:53,817 --> 00:09:56,282
dressed in their best clothes for the

294
00:09:56,282 --> 00:09:56,881
airplane.

295
00:09:57,061 --> 00:09:58,326
But most drove.

296
00:09:58,506 --> 00:10:00,620
A week away was a luxury.

297
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:02,495
Then it was back to work on

298
00:10:02,495 --> 00:10:04,379
Monday, with a new ring on the

299
00:10:04,379 --> 00:10:06,137
finger and a new name on the

300
00:10:06,137 --> 00:10:06,639
mailbox.

301
00:10:06,990 --> 00:10:09,068
The first home was often small.

302
00:10:09,248 --> 00:10:11,609
A rented apartment over a store,

303
00:10:11,609 --> 00:10:14,118
a few rooms in a two-family house,

304
00:10:14,118 --> 00:10:16,552
or even a bedroom in her parents'

305
00:10:16,552 --> 00:10:18,397
house for the first year,

306
00:10:18,397 --> 00:10:20,463
until they had saved enough.

307
00:10:20,643 --> 00:10:23,736
The furniture was secondhand or given by

308
00:10:23,736 --> 00:10:24,509
relatives.

309
00:10:24,689 --> 00:10:27,046
A card table served as the dining

310
00:10:27,046 --> 00:10:29,616
table until they could afford a real

311
00:10:29,616 --> 00:10:29,902
one.

312
00:10:30,252 --> 00:10:32,338
The new wife learned to cook from

313
00:10:32,338 --> 00:10:34,297
a cookbook given at the shower,

314
00:10:34,297 --> 00:10:36,509
often the same one her mother used.

315
00:10:36,689 --> 00:10:38,949
The new husband learned to fix the

316
00:10:38,949 --> 00:10:39,812
leaky faucet.

317
00:10:39,992 --> 00:10:42,042
And both of them learned to live

318
00:10:42,042 --> 00:10:43,452
on one small paycheck,

319
00:10:43,452 --> 00:10:46,142
counting every dollar at the kitchen table

320
00:10:46,142 --> 00:10:47,167
on Friday night.

321
00:10:47,518 --> 00:10:51,023
Married life in nineteen sixty-five also meant

322
00:10:51,023 --> 00:10:52,243
a lot of firsts.

323
00:10:52,423 --> 00:10:53,851
The first fight about money.

324
00:10:54,031 --> 00:10:56,634
The first Thanksgiving hosted in their own

325
00:10:56,634 --> 00:10:58,988
apartment, with both mothers watching.

326
00:10:59,168 --> 00:11:01,196
The first time she wrote her new

327
00:11:01,196 --> 00:11:02,210
name on a check.

328
00:11:02,390 --> 00:11:04,247
The first time he came home and

329
00:11:04,247 --> 00:11:06,104
there was a hot dinner waiting,

330
00:11:06,104 --> 00:11:06,883
just for him.

331
00:11:07,234 --> 00:11:08,785
Within a year or two,

332
00:11:08,785 --> 00:11:11,297
many couples had their first baby.

333
00:11:11,477 --> 00:11:12,652
Within five years,

334
00:11:12,652 --> 00:11:14,806
many had bought a little house of

335
00:11:14,806 --> 00:11:15,459
their own.

336
00:11:15,639 --> 00:11:18,271
The wedding photographs went into an album

337
00:11:18,271 --> 00:11:20,526
with a white cover and gold letters,

338
00:11:20,526 --> 00:11:22,406
and the album went on a shelf,

339
00:11:22,406 --> 00:11:24,598
where it waited for the children to

340
00:11:24,598 --> 00:11:25,601
find it someday.

341
00:11:25,951 --> 00:11:29,106
Why do weddings from nineteen sixty-five feel

342
00:11:29,106 --> 00:11:31,140
so special when we look back?

343
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,039
Maybe because they were simpler.

344
00:11:33,219 --> 00:11:34,658
A church, a hall,

345
00:11:34,658 --> 00:11:36,945
a cake and a record player.

346
00:11:37,125 --> 00:11:39,505
Nobody went into debt for a wedding.

347
00:11:39,685 --> 00:11:41,371
The whole neighborhood helped,

348
00:11:41,371 --> 00:11:43,168
and the whole neighborhood came.

349
00:11:43,519 --> 00:11:45,806
Maybe because it truly was a beginning.

350
00:11:45,986 --> 00:11:48,738
These were young people starting with almost

351
00:11:48,738 --> 00:11:51,428
nothing, and building a whole life together

352
00:11:51,428 --> 00:11:52,116
from there.

353
00:11:52,296 --> 00:11:55,341
Some of those marriages lasted fifty or

354
00:11:55,341 --> 00:11:56,278
sixty years.

355
00:11:56,458 --> 00:11:58,773
And some of those couples are still

356
00:11:58,773 --> 00:12:00,097
holding hands today,

357
00:12:00,097 --> 00:12:02,610
looking at that same photograph on the

358
00:12:02,610 --> 00:12:03,470
church steps.

359
00:12:03,821 --> 00:12:05,701
So tell us in the comments.

360
00:12:05,881 --> 00:12:07,439
What year did you get married,

361
00:12:07,439 --> 00:12:08,892
and where did you go on your

362
00:12:08,892 --> 00:12:09,411
honeymoon?

363
00:12:09,591 --> 00:12:11,522
We read every single comment,

364
00:12:11,522 --> 00:12:13,852
and we'd love to hear your stories.

365
00:12:14,032 --> 00:12:16,431
Next, we'll find out what happened to

366
00:12:16,431 --> 00:12:17,793
the American milkman.

367
00:12:17,973 --> 00:12:19,799
Thank you for spending this time with

368
00:12:19,799 --> 00:12:19,947
me.
